---
description: Update AIMetrics block in file headers for all modified or created files
alwaysApply: true
---

Every file you create or modify must have an AIMetrics block in its file header comment.

- If a file already has an AIMetrics block, update it by adding a new entry to the array (do not remove existing entries).
- If a file has no AIMetrics block, add one.
- For new files created entirely by the agent, set contribution value to 100.
- For modified files, estimate the percentage of lines changed and use that as the contribution value.

**Exempt files** carry no AIMetrics block, because they carry no header at all
(see the `licence-header` rule for the full list): dotfiles such as
`.gitignore`; lockfiles and machine-generated files; and the imported
`agents/rules/*.mdc` themselves.

Each entry records one agent/model contribution to the file:

- `timestamp` — the date and time of the modification, ISO 8601 format (YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss).
- `name` — the name of the agent or tool that made the modification. This must identify the
  agent running the current session (e.g. the harness package name, such as `@deepseek-ai/dsh`).
  Do NOT copy the values from the examples below; they are illustrative only.
- `version` — the version of that agent/tool, taken from the current session (e.g. `0.2.0-rc.2`).
- `model` — the identifier of the model powering the agent in the current session
  (e.g. `deepseek-flash`).
- `contribution.value` — the percentage of the file's lines changed by this contribution
  (100 for a new file).

The `name`, `version`, and `model` fields must always describe the agent and model that
actually performed the modification. In this project that is normally `@deepseek-ai/dsh`,
the version reported by `dsh --version`, and the `model` from the `agent-default-model`
entry of the active profile (see `$DSH_HOME/profiles/<profile>/cordis.patch.yml`).

The format for JavaScript and Node files (`.mjs`, `.js`, `.cjs`), in `//` comments, is:

```
// AIMetrics: [
//     {
//     "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
//     "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
//     "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
//     "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
//     "contribution": {
//       "value": <percentage>,
//       "units": "%"
//       }
//     }
//   ]
```

The format for Python, YAML, shell, and other `#`-commented files is:

```
# AIMetrics: [
#     {
#     "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
#     "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
#     "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
#     "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
#     "contribution": {
#       "value": <percentage>,
#       "units": "%"
#       }
#     }
#   ]
```

The format for Java files (inside the `/* */` header comment) is:

```
 * AIMetrics: [
 *     {
 *     "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
 *     "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
 *     "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
 *     "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
 *     "contribution": {
 *       "value": <percentage>,
 *       "units": "%"
 *       }
 *     }
 *   ]
```

The format for Markdown files and XML files (inside `<!-- -->` comments) is:

```
  AIMetrics: [
      {
      "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
      "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
      "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
      "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
      "contribution": {
        "value": <percentage>,
        "units": "%"
        }
      }
    ]
```

Place the block after the `</meta:header>` tag and before the closing comment delimiter.

For files with no AIMetrics or AI contribution, the block should be:

```
 * AIMetrics: []
```

(using the appropriate comment prefix for the file type).

> **Imported from the Calycopis-broker project**
> (`/Calycopis/Calycopis-broker/agents/rules/ai-metrics.mdc`) and adapted for this
> project: the JavaScript and Markdown formats have been added, because this
> project is mostly `.mjs` and `.md`, and the exemption above now also covers
> dotfiles and machine-generated files rather than only `agents/rules/*.mdc`.

### Git commit messages

Every git commit message created by an agent must end with an AIMetrics block.

- The `name`, `version`, and `model` fields are the same as the values used for a file header
  (see above): they must always describe the agent and model that actually performed the work.
- The `contribution.value` is an estimate of how much of the changes in the commit were
  contributed by the agent. If all of the changes were contributed by the agent, set the value
  to `100`.
- A commit message block uses `interval` instead of `timestamp`: an ISO 8601 interval covering
  the current session (e.g. `2026-02-14T15:30:00/2026-03-14T05:00:00`).

The format for an AIMetrics block at the end of a commit message is:

```
AIMetrics: [
    {
    "interval": "<ISO 8601 interval covering the current session, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00/2026-03-14T05:00:00>",
    "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
    "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
    "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
    "contribution": {
      "value": <percentage>,
      "units": "%"
      }
    }
  ]
```

### GitHub issues

Every GitHub issue created by an agent must end with an AIMetrics block, for
the same reason a commit message does: the issue is an authored artifact, and
the block records which agent and model produced it.

- The `name`, `version`, and `model` fields are the same as the values used for
  a file header and a commit message: they must always describe the agent and
  model that actually performed the work.
- Use `timestamp`, as in a file header, rather than the `interval` a commit
  message uses. A commit message summarises a session's work; an issue is
  created at a point in time. If an agent later edits the issue body, append a
  new entry rather than replacing existing ones, exactly as for a file header.
- The `contribution.value` is `100` for an issue the agent created in full, and
  an estimate of the lines changed for one it later edited.
- Surround the block with a fenced code block, because an issue body is Markdown
  and has no comment syntax to put it in. It must be the last thing in the body.

The format for an AIMetrics block at the end of a GitHub issue body is:

````text
```text
AIMetrics: [
    {
    "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the issue, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
    "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
    "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
    "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
    "contribution": {
      "value": <percentage>,
      "units": "%"
      }
    }
  ]
```
````
